Exact form (error ≤2.2e−16): E(d) = √2·(1+u²)^(−1/2)·exp(−(σ0²/2)·u²/(1+u²)), where u = d/σ0². In general u = ħd/(mσ0²), and the plateau is exp(−(mσ0/ħ)²/2).
E is strictly decreasing: d lnE/du = −u/(1+u²) − σ0²u/(1+u²)² < 0; in mpmath max dE/dd < 0. Non-decreasing steps in float64 occur only among subnormal values ~1e−322.
There are two scales. For d ≪ σ0² E is a Gaussian exp(−d²(1+σ0²)/(2σ0⁴)). For d ≫ σ0² the Gaussian factor reaches the plateau e^{−σ0²/2}, and E ≈ √2·σ0²·e^{−σ0²/2}/d; the ratio to this asymptote at d=1e7 is 1.000000 for all σ0.
