In float32 at σ0=20 E becomes exactly 0 from d=417, and the weight (1·E)² from d=238.
The tail coefficient √2·σ0²·e^{−σ0²/2} is largest at σ0=√2 (1.04); at σ0=5 it is 1.3e−4, at σ0=20 7.8e−85.

Weight distribution of the last query at equal |q·k|, T=128, weight = E²:
σ0=0.3: on itself 0.988, d≥32: 1.7e−4;
σ0=0.5: on itself 0.928, d≥32: 1.1e−3;
σ0=1: on itself 0.651, d≥32: 5.7e−3;
σ0=1.41: d≥32: 6.6e−3;
σ0=2 (init): on itself 0.41, d≤4: 0.953, d≥32: 2.9e−3;
σ0=4: median d=2, d≥32: 1.0e−6;
σ0=5: on itself 0.20, d≥32: 4.6e−8;
σ0=20: median d=9, d≥32: 2.6e−2;
σ0=50: d≥32: 0.37 (a uniform weight would give 0.75).
The dependence on σ0 is non-monotonic. The “narrow” head looks almost entirely at its own token, and its weight tail is a power law, ~1/d². At σ0=4–4.5, i.e. for the “wide global” head of v1, it is a window of 2–3 tokens with a tail of 1e−6…1e−8. At long distances the v1 pair (σ0=0.5 and 4) is ordered opposite to Annex A: the share at d≥32 is 1.1e−3 vs 1.0e−6.
A wide window appears only at σ0≳20, and it is a static Gaussian window exp(−d²/2σ0²): the TV distance of the row to it is 8e−4 at σ0=20 and 1e−4 at σ0=50, so dispersion does not affect the result.
For the narrow head, 92–99% of the decay comes from the prefactor −log(1+u²), i.e. the drop in height of the spreading packet, not from the Gaussian factor: at σ0=0.5, d=127 the contributions are −12.46 and −0.25 nats.
For comparison, at the same integer positions: under the paper's formula (c) the narrow head gets an almost uniform weight (on itself 0.009, median d=63). The physically correct (b) gives a static window of width ~σ0 (at σ0=0.3, on itself 0.996). This matches the description in Annex A, but there is no dispersion in it.
